                       COMPUTER USER CODE OF ETHICS 
 
       The computing systems at Queen's University are intended to support 
its educational purposes and to enhance its educational environment.  All 
Queen's computer users should be aware both that they have access to 
valuable and sensitive resources and that their computing practices can 
adversely affect others.
 
       In the following, "computing" includes any personal computer, 
computing or communications facility operated wholly or partly within or 
from Queen's University; "account" means any account number, access code, 
userid or authorization code for a computer or communications facility, 
together with its associated passwords and files; "user" means any person 
using Queen's computing facilities: "system administrator" means the person 
who has the authority to grant authorization to use computing facilities.
 
       The list below constitutes a Computer User Code of Ethics for 
Queen's University.  Disciplinary action for violating the Code shall be 
governed by the applicable provisions of the Code of Conduct or other 
relevant policy of the university.
 
1.     Users must use only those computing facilities and accounts for 
       which they have proper authorization.  Users are responsible for
       the use of their computing accounts.  They should maintain secure
       passwords for systems that support them and take precautions 
       against others obtaining unauthorized access to their computing 
       resources.  This obligation to provide sufficient protection applies
       particularly to users who are responsible for confidential 
      information.
 
2.     Users must use computing facilities and services only for the 
       purposes for which they were authorized.  Specifically, 
       Computing and Communications Services' research and instructional 
       accounts must not be used for private consulting or sold to other
       individuals; use of any part of the computing facility for direct 
       personal financial gain (except for appropriate contract and 
       external accounts) or to provide free resources for unauthorized
       purposes is not allowed.
 
3.     Users must not attempt to access or copy the programs and 
       information belonging to or licensed to other users or to Queen's
       unless they have proper authorization to do so.  Programs,
       subroutines and data may not be moved from one computing system
       to another without permission.  Users are reminded that this applies
       equally to microcomputer software.  The terms and conditions in
       software licenses vary considerably.  The onus is on users/owners
       to familiarize themselves with their responsibilities and 
       limitations under each agreement.
 
4.     Users must not attempt to interfere with the normal operation of a 
       shared system.
 
5.     Users must not attempt to encroach on others' use of computing
       facilities or to deprive them of resources.
 
6.     Users must not attempt to subvert the restrictions associated with 
       their computing accounts.
 
7.     Users must not use computing facilities to send obscene, vulgar or
       harassing messages.
 
8.     Users must not attempt unauthorized access of computing
       installations outside of Queen's using Queen's computers
       or communications facilities.
 
       Although system administrators may attempt to provide and preserve 
security of files, account numbers, passwords and programs, it is possible 
that security can be breached through action or causes beyond reasonable 
control.  Users are therefore urged to safeguard data and to take full 
advantage of file security mechanisms built into systems (for example by 
changing passwords as often as necessary).  System administrators of shared 
facilities have a responsibility to inform users of their obligations in 
the use of these systems.
 
                                 
                                    ***
 
 
      DISCIPLINARY PROCEDURES FOR CASES OF COMPUTER ABUSE - JAN 9, 1986
 
This document presents the disciplinary steps to be taken by Computing 
Services when individuals violate the "Computer User Code of Ethics for 
Queen's University".  The steps provide for an increasing severity of 
disciplinary action for successive violations.
 
The disciplinary procedures are as follows:
 
1.  First violation will result in an electronic warning to cease the 
    current abuse .
 
2.  Second violation will result in a verbal and written warning, copied to
    appropriate department heads and deans.
 
3.  Third violation will result in a restriction in access to the 
    facilities to non-prime time shifts.  This restriction will be in 
    effect for the equivalent of one full academic term and may span
    academic years.
 
4.  Fourth violation will result in complete suspension of access to the 
    computing facilities for the equivalent of at least one full academic
    term.
                                
In extreme cases of abuse or if major damage has occurred the process will 
be accelerated.
 
The above actions do not preclude standard university disciplinary action 
being invoked.
 

 

