  INTRODUCTION:
  _____________
 
  In  the  autumn  of  1988,  the  Senate   Computing and Com-
  munications Committee began a detailed evaluation of  future
  computing  requirements  for Queen's University.  This eval-
  uation was conducted as a two stage process with two subcom-
  mittees being struck; one to examine the NEEDS as  perceived
  by the university community and the other to develop  STRAT-
  EGIES to satisfy those needs.  Each subcommittee was made up
  of  members  of  the  Senate Computing & Communications Com-
  mittee with additional members  seconded  from  outside  the
  committee.  The following report is the result of those com-
  bined investigations.
 
  The Needs subcommittee conducted a survey in the  spring  of
  1989 to determine what the university community perceived as
  its  needs  for  future  development of computing resources.
  The subcommittee also met with groups from  departments  and
  faculties across the university. The resulting report of the
  needs  subcommittee  to  the  Senate  Computing  and  Commu-
  nications Committee [Appendix B] emphasizes  one  key  fact.
  There  is  enormous  and  increasing diversity in what we at
  Queen's University think we need.  At one extreme, there are
  some  who  do no computing now and see no need for it in the
  future. At the other extreme, are those individuals who cur-
  rently use a significant fraction of the available power  of
  the  central academic mainframe and lament at how slow it is
  and how little access they get to it. Between these extremes
  are those who prepare documents with word processors, manage
  numbers with spreadsheets, manipulate information with  data
  base  managers,  communicate  with  colleagues by electronic
  mail, and manage research data with  statistical  and  other
  discipline-specific  programs.  The actual equipment used by
  individuals range from simple  terminals  to  personal  com-
  puters  to high-performance workstations.  A common theme of
  the responses to the needs survey was the concept  of  doing
  more of the same but doing it more easily, more quickly, and
  more cheaply. The diversity of computing needs is a strength
  of  any modern university but supporting this diversity will
  be neither simple nor inexpensive.
 
  In June 1989, a Queen's Gazette supplement entitled "Towards
  a Strategy For Computing at Queen's" was  published  by  the
  Strategies  subcommittee  to  generate  discussion about di-
  rections the university might take  in  central  support  of
  computing.  The 'Towards' document introduced the concept of
  Scholar-Centred Computing in which the primary objective be-
  comes  the delivery of effective computer-based applications
  to the desktop. The computer becomes a personal helper,  as-
  sisting in information gathering, analysis, and presentation
  as  well  as  a  wide  range of personal productivity appli-
  cations. The computer as a window on the world is a  logical
  extension  of the networked computing which is developing in
  some departments today.   The infrastructure  for  this  new
  generation  of  computing  is  gradually evolving across the
  campus. Of particular importance is the optical fibre  based
  campus  backbone  network which interconnects local networks
  within buildings to each other, to  central  mainframe  com-
  puters,  and  beyond to national and international networks.
  The ability to move information quickly and easily is essen-
  tial to Scholar-Centred Computing. Ideally, one of  the cen-
  tral resources provided would be high speed  data  links  to
  every  desktop in the university including those of faculty,
  support staff, and students. In such a  user-centred  model,
  the  actual  computer on the desk must have sufficient power
  and screen resolution to support a convenient  and  easy-to-
  use  interface  to  applications  programs  but the computer
  which stores and manages the information may be remote  from
  the  user  and  need  not even be at the university. In many
  leading universities around the world, development  of  high
  speed  university  and inter-university data networks is al-
  ready well under way as a high priority in support  of  both
  teaching and research.
 
  It  would  be  wrong to think that, because such a computing
  system is highly distributed, it does not  require  central-
  ized  resources.  It  is important to recognize that central
  support is essential for the effective use of computing  re-
  sources.  The current program of microcomputing support from
  within Computing & Communications Services (C&CS) provides a
  strong base for  those  new  to  computing.  Even  in  those
  departments  which  have  their  own  microcomputing support
  personnel, there are good contacts between the local experts
  and C&CS support staff.  Those departments with  workstation
  networks  are,  at  the  moment,  providing their own system
  administration. In most cases, this means that not only  are
  these  departments  providing  computing  hardware, they are
  also using departmental resources  to  run  what  amount  to
  their  own  computing centres. This may not be efficient nor
  cost-effective and only a few departments may be able to af-
  ford it.  Interconnected computers are clearly  one  of  the
  most  important  computing  requirements  of the future, and
  central support of these networks is essential to bring them
  into wide use across the university.  At the same  time,  it
  is  important that upgrading support continue to be provided
  for those users for  whom  a  stand-alone  microcomputer  is
  sufficient.
 
  Computers  have  become useful tools for teaching, research,
  and personal information management across the entire  spec-
  trum  of  academic disciplines.  We have a responsibility to
  move forward in these areas.   In  the  1970's,  individuals
  used  typewriters to prepare documents.  In the 1980's, most
  of us graduated to microcomputers for document  preparation.
  The  power  of  the  microcomputer  allowed us to expand our
  information management to include spreadsheet  and  database
  analysis  of  files located on our computers. In the 1990's,
  we will see another significant expansion of our ability  to
  use computers for information management. Through the use of
  networks,  we  will  no longer be limited to data on our own
  computers.  The computer on the desktop  will  allow  us  to
  move documents electronically anywhere in the world. We will
  conduct electronic transfers of memoranda and letters around
  departments, around the university, and beyond. We will con-
  duct  literature searches and examine other databases within
  our own libraries and at remote locations. We  will  connect
  to  computers  at remote sites to perform specialized compu-
  tations.   With proper support, the  desktop  computer  will
  provide  a  convenient  interface  to all of these functions
  without the user having to learn any  new  programs.  It  is
  vitally important that the near term planning be flexible so
  that  our  use  of  computing can evolve smoothly to deliver
  computing power as widely as possible.
 
  The Strategy detailed below is intended as a set  of  objec-
  tives  to  bring computing support in line with the goals of
  Scholar-Centred Computing.  These objectives are set out  in
  a  series of recommendations for broad support of a range of
  computing tasks on a range of computing hardware.  There  is
  no single solution. We must strengthen the support for indi-
  viduals  working  with  specific programs, we must assist in
  the choice of hardware for new applications, and we must ex-
  pand and simplify interconnections through campus  networks.
  Public  sites must be upgraded on a continuing basis to pro-
  vide access to computing resources for those scholars who do
  not have their own computers.  In order that information  be
  available  in reasonable time, some upgrades of central com-
  puters will be required and it will be necessary to  modern-
  ize  the  storage  and  access  to  those  files  through  a
  university-wide file system. This report is designed to  al-
  low  an  orderly  and  flexible development of the computing
  environment for the 1990's at Queen's University.
 
  A  complication in planning for the future lies in the blur-
  ring of roles between 'academic' and  'administrative'  com-
  puting.  Even  now,  users  who  are  clearly administrative
  employees, such as those in the Office of the Registrar, use
  the statistical software on the academic computer to analyse
  records. Individual faculty  members  routinely  use  online
  information from the student records data base when advising
  students.  The  management  of  the library database is cur-
  rently treated as an 'administrative' function  yet  no  one
  would  argue  that  access  to library information is not an
  essential academic service provided by a university  to  its
  staff  and students. The final recommendation of this report
  is that a mechanism be found to ensure that there be  paral-
  lel  and  integrated  development  of  academic and adminis-
  trative computing in the university.
 
 
 
 
 
  CURRENT STATUS:
  _______________
 
  Queen's enters this next  phase  of  computing  development,
  that  is  Scholar-Centred  Computing,  from  a  position  of
  strength.  Over the past several years, Queen's has  evolved
  its computing environment by
 
  o   strongly  recommending  that  students acquire their own
      microcomputers (initiated by the Faculty of Applied Sci-
      ence in 1985)
 
  o   creating a fibre-optic based campus wide  backbone  net-
      work  and  providing  communications  access to external
      networks
 
  o   selecting a high-powered  workstation  of  choice  (SCCC
      Subcommittee, 1988)
 
  o   developing  the  computing  infrastructure to complement
      the above growth activities.
 
  As stated in the SCCC '87 REVISED POLICY FOR  ACADEMIC  COM-
  PUTING AT QUEEN'S UNIVERSITY, these steps were undertaken to
  ensure that computing is:
 
  o   A service tool in all academic fields
  o   A tool for research
  o   A subject of study and instruction
  o   A tool in teaching
  o   A tool in information handling.
 
  While  the  above objectives are still relevant in 1990, the
  variety and complexity of the Queen's computing  environment
  has changed considerably since 1987.
 
  Several  comments  made to the Needs Subcommittee and on the
  discussion paper (TOWARDS) regarding the  proposed  Scholar-
  Centred  Computing direction, indicated dissatisfaction with
  a perceived lack of attention to:
 
  o   the support of microcomputers within Queen's
 
  o   the use of microcomputers for classroom instruction
 
  o   the relationship between  administrative  computing  and
      Scholar-Centred Computing.
 
  This  report  will  comment  on these and other observations
  identified in the report produced by the Needs  Subcommittee
  (the Needs Report is attached as Appendix B).
 
 
 
 
  SCHOLAR-CENTRED COMPUTING:
  __________________________
 
  Scholar-Centred  Computing  is  to some extent an ideal - if
  hardware and software availability and cost were not issues,
  what would be the most productive way to implement computing
  in a scholar's life?
 
  The previous model of computing was that of a large,  expen-
  sive  mainframe  fed  by moderately decentralized terminals.
  Most of the hardware was expensive and fixed in place.  Most
  of the software was specialized and inflexible, and required
  considerable experience and perseverance to use.  The  style
  of  interaction  with each type of computer system tended to
  be highly unique, such that it required  considerable  over-
  head in time and ingenuity to learn how to use more than one
  system.
 
  Taken  together,  these facts dictated that a scholar had to
  shape his/her approach and questions to match  the  specifi-
  cations  of  a  particular university computing environment.
  The idiosyncrasies of the computing environment took  prece-
  dence over the intellectual content.
 
  In  Scholar-Centred  Computing,  the  particulars of a given
  hardware and software configuration cease to have any impor-
  tance for the user.   Indeed, the "workstation"  becomes  an
  environment tailored to the needs of each and every scholar,
  such  that  they can arrange to interact with the machine in
  whatever manner feels most natural or productive  for  them.
  In  other words, the computing environment is moulded to fit
  a scholar's work style or question, rather than vice versa.
 
  In practical terms, this means that different  scholars,  in
  different disciplines, may have radically different specific
  types  of  workstations.   Yet all will share certain common
  features such as connectivity and at least one level of com-
  mand interface (eg. a "standard desktop" with  generic  win-
  dows   and   pull-down  menus).  All  workstations  will  be
  immediately useful without  a  manual  or  training  on  the
  specific  hardware  involved,  and  all  will  be  adaptable
  (within limits) to suit more specific needs without special-
  ist intervention.  As an example, if UNIX was a standardized
  operating system, one could imagine running  a  personalized
  "personality  module"  that  could turn anything from a "DOS
  PC", a Macintosh,  a  SUN,  or  a  mainframe  terminal  into
  exactly  the  standard  desktop,  with the same files in the
  same arrangement, with the  same  pattern  of  connectivity.
  This  would hold whether one was at the machine in one's of-
  fice, or a colleague's office, a public site, or ideally  in
  a lab on another continent.
 
  In  real terms such a "seamless" environment means that most
  participating workstations have:
 
  o   a powerful processor
  o   high resolution and fast graphics capability
  o   high speed network links
  o   very smart operating system software
  o   standards for  communication  protocols,  windowing  and
      terminal emulation and
  o   access to a wide diversity of software (able to run on a
      wide variety of hardware).
 
 
  OBSERVATIONS:
  _____________
 
  At  Queen's  and  other  universities  the  major  computing
  constituencies revolve around
 
  o   Education
  o   Research
  o   Administration
  o   Personal productivity.
 
  Queen's central computing  resources  are  in  a  transition
  phase,  moving  to a more distributed computing environment.
  But distributed computing is more than timesharing  systems,
  electronic  mail, and microcomputers with floppies.  Queen's
  distributed computing approach must be adaptive to an always
  changing environment, and will be based on
 
  o   fully networked systems and services
  o   computing in the hands of the end-user
  o   a coherent computing environment, campus-wide
  o   supporting collaborative work
 
  which is the essence of Scholar-Centred Computing.
  Queen's has been implementing the components of  a  distrib-
  uted computing environment in a planned, controlled fashion.
  These  building  blocks  include networks, end-user systems,
  server systems, and support services.
 
 
  ISSUES:
  _______
 
  1-  SUPPORTING DIVERSITY: A BALANCED APPROACH
 
  There  still  exists  a  wide range of computer literacy and
  utilization within the University.  This is reflected in the
  diversity of applications, from word processing to those re-
  quiring the processing power of a  supercomputer.    No  one
  technology  is appropriate for all applications, and in this
  era of transition, it would be a serious  mistake  to  focus
  too  much attention on either extreme. The guiding principle
  should be:  select the appropriate technology (ie. the  best
  system) for the application.
 
  Today,  for  many purposes, microcomputers are evolving into
  workstations,  particularly  if  they  are   networked   and
  386-based.  As  the  technology  of  microcomputers develops
  (especially  microcomputer  operating  systems),  the   dis-
  tinction between microcomputers and workstations will become
  even less relevant.
 
  The  University, as a step to achieving its goal of Scholar-
  Centred Computing, must continue  to  support  computing  at
  Queen's  on  all levels:  microcomputers, high-powered work-
  stations and central resources, including networking.
 
 
  2-  ADDITIONAL SPECIALIZED SUPPORT STAFF
 
  The  following  charts  clearly  demonstrate the significant
  growth of the number of computers within the University.
 
 
          - Charts:  "Net Sun Workstation Sales at Queen's
            University"  and  PC Systems Sold by Computing
            Services  at Queen's University"  available in
            the Senate Office -
 
 
  Assuming  that  50%  of  the microcomputer sales are in fact
  University purchases, then the Queen's community  has  spent
  in the past four years in excess of $12,000,000 on microcom-
  puters and workstations.
 
  This  rapid  growth  of the new user community has created a
  conflicting mixture of demands, from more  training  courses
  and  more technical support on an ever increasing variety of
  products, to an expectation that all existing services  will
  continue to be provided.  For example, the SCCC High-powered
  Workstation  Subcommittee  report  recommended  that support
  services for workstations be provided to the same extent  as
  in  the  DOS  / PC environment.  While re-assignment of C&CS
  staff to respond to this type of  request  is  a  continuing
  activity, the additional workload makes it impossible to add
  new  user  services  without  additional specialized support
  staff.
 
  New roles requiring the creation of  new  positions  include
  user training on new products, technical support for sophis-
  ticated  application systems, network and distributed system
  management support, and operating systems.
 
  The common thread to our diverse needs is a pool of skilled,
  articulate and accessible people within Computing and Commu-
  nications Services (C&CS).  The University must plan to  in-
  crease  the  staffing  complement of C&CS by at least 4 over
  the next four years.
 
 
  3-  NETWORK EXPANSION
 
  Connectivity,  as  important  as  it is now, will be a major
  component of future computing.   In  order  for  Queen's  to
  optimize  its already extensive investment in microcomputers
  and workstations, it  will  be  necessary  to  continue  the
  development  of our communications networks.  This means not
  only installing additional optical fibre to serve all campus
  buildings, but encouraging and supporting the active  growth
  of network users. C&CS must take a leadership role in estab-
  lishing  networking  protocols to ensure connectivity and in
  advising / informing users of the networking options  avail-
  able.  Networking must be demystified and simplified.
 
  Hence  it is imperative that the University continue to sup-
  port the expansion and  use  of  campus-wide  communications
  networks,  including local area networks, and connections to
  external networks.
 
 
  4-  MAINTAINING ACCESSIBILITY TO COMPUTING RESOURCES
 
  Funding  to  provide general-access student workstations for
  teaching and research should continue  to  be  a  university
  responsibility. Additional departments will be following the
  Faculty  of  Applied  Science model of strongly recommending
  that students acquire their own systems.  However, many stu-
  dents will still be very dependent on the centrally provided
  public and semi-public  sites.    As  departmental  teaching
  requirements  change,  putting new demands on the equipment,
  these sites must be upgraded.
 
  It is recommended that the University  continue  to  support
  both public and semi-public site renewal, ensuring universal
  access  to state-of-the-art equipment, applications and net-
  work resources, and providing improved facilities for  group
  instruction. The expenditure of these funds will continue to
  be  a  consultative  effort  between  C&CS,  the appropriate
  department, and the SCCC.
 
 
  5-  LIBRARY GROWTH
 
  The computer is a tool essential to the  effective  use  and
  operation  of  a  modern research library.   Computer commu-
  nications and network systems enable the provision of  accu-
  rate  and  timely  information  regarding  the  acquisition,
  location, and status of the intellectual materials available
  within  the  Queen's  academic community, and access to very
  large depositories of machine-readable information stored at
  various sites within Ontario,  Canada,  North  America,  and
  throughout the world.
 
  Users  of  library  systems  expect  that the system will be
  available wherever and whenever the need for it arises.
 
  It will not be long before library patrons expect documents,
  not merely bibliographic information,  to  be  delivered  in
  electronic form. Although general document delivery is prob-
  ably  not  viable  now,  we should be looking forward to the
  possibilities of journals in electronic form in the near fu-
  ture.  In particular, optical storage systems will become  a
  key  component for document storage. The library's computing
  needs are in essence more of the same plus the potential  to
  provide electronic document delivery.
 
  The  provision  of  future  library  services to the Queen's
  community will require the University to provide  additional
  support  for more library mass storage, appropriate process-
  ing capacity to maintain sub-second response rate, a  trans-
  parent  interface  with  external  databases,  and  superior
  connections to local networks, external databases and  other
  libraries.
 
 
  6-  NETWORK MANAGEMENT AND SERVICES
 
  The  communications  strategy for Queen's has been  evolving
  with the development of the campus-wide backbone network and
  departmental  local  area  networks  (LAN).  However,   some
  departments  have  found  the  ongoing  network   management
  requirements to be a problem.
 
  The goal is to establish a Queen's File System (QFS) service
  capable  of supporting thousands of workstations,  providing
  file storage and retrieval systems.  This user-driven system
  would be transparent to the user -- the data would appear to
  be  resident on a local hard disk.  Users who choose to  use
  this service could receive network management functions such
  as file backups, archive services, file services  --  fetch/
  store,  etc.; network standards would also  be  administered
  centrally.  As  well, additional services such  as  network-
  based operating system downloads, user consulting and utili-
  ties could be provided.
 
  The following diagram presents a simplified view of what the
  environment would look like.
 
          - Diagram "Proposed Queen's File System" availabe in
            the Senate Office. -
 
  C&CS should investigate the implementation of a Queen's File
  System service.
 
 
  7-  MAINFRAMES IN TRANSITION
 
  The   charts   ("Net  Sun  Workstation  Sales   at   Queen's
  University"  and "PC Systems Sold by Computing  Services  at
  Queen's University") on page 7 of this report clearly indic-
  ate the huge growth of both microcomputers and  workstations
  at Queen's.  However, in the same time frame, the demand for
  centrally  provided computer resources has continued to  in-
  crease  (see Appendix C). Almost without exception,  depart-
  ments have maintained their utilization of the central faci-
  lity.  In many cases there are the same departments who have
  been acquiring large numbers of microcomputers and/or  work-
  stations.  In  many cases this increase has  been  dramatic:
  for  example,  Arts and Science usage has  grown  five  fold
  since 83/84.  Evidently the acquisition of these  additional
  computing  resources  has  not lessened  the  demand  for  a
  strong, reliable central facility.
 
     - Chart "Total Mainframe Computing Charges" available  in
       the Senate Office. -
 
  There  remains a strong requirement to continue  to  provide
  general purpose computing "cycles" for a diverse and signif-
  icant  group.  This includes researchers who need access  to
  computing  which  exceeds their research  grants.   Graduate
  students  require  access  to large amounts of computing re-
  sources for instruction and thesis research, often utilizing
  expensive   software  sometimes  acquired  through   special
  arrangements  with suppliers who demand security  for  their
  products.  Undergraduate students use an effective  instruc-
  tional  computing environment including access to  software,
  test data and other course-related materials.  Currently  we
  have over 6,000 users accessing the academic mainframe.
 
  For  some  research programs, only access  to  a  CRAY-level
  supercomputer would be appropriate.  Funding for such access
  will continue to be subsidized by our networking  activities
  (eg.  ONet,  NRnet), supercomputer grants,  and  perhaps  by
  individual research grants.
 
  However,  as  the academic mainframe (acquired in  1984)  is
  struggling  to meet current levels of usage, any  additional
  or new requirements would be impossible.
 
  This implies that:
 
  o  new services such as the previously recommended QFS  will
     be delayed
 
  o  existing users will notice a continually declining system
     performance
 
  o  the transition from a VM/CMS operating environment to  a
     new platform (eg. UNIX) will not be possible
 
  It  should  be noted that similar constraints will  soon  be
  placed on the administrative mainframe, currently supporting
  the library system and the administrative computing environ-
  ment.  The library in particular will be requiring excellent
  networking linkages and extensive amounts of additional disk
  storage.
 
  Therefore, C&CS should, as part of its planning process  and
  in  conjunction with the SCCC, investigate the  options  for
  upgrading  the  central  facility  to  respond  to  existing
  demands, and to accommodate new applications / environments.
 
 
  8-  NEW INITIATIVES REQUIRING UNIVERSITY SUPPORT
 
  To encourage Queen's to move towards Scholar-Centred Comput-
  ing, increased information technology support for previously
  unfunded activities must be found, but not at the expense of
  the  centrally supported resources.  New activities such  as
  the  provision of routers to facilitate departmental  access
  to the campus backbone network, or a subsidy for faculty who
  purchase "institute approved hardware", or the provision  of
  data  connections to every faculty member's  computer,  fall
  into this category.
 
  It is recommended that the University take the initiative in
  supporting  new  computing approaches  which  encourage  the
  development of Scholar-Centred Computing.
 
 
  The Integration of Academic and Administrative Computing
 
  The  University's financial and student records systems  are
  examples of systems that are critical to Queen's  operation.
  Both  illustrate  Queen's use of commercially  available  or
  "homegrown"  software.  Many of our existing  administrative
  systems  were implemented several years ago and have  under-
  gone enhancements and refinements.  These changes have  been
  made, over a period of 8 - 10 years, by  different  analysts
  and  programmers, and contain different  programming  styles
  and standards.  These systems are complex and  consequently,
  it  is  very  time consuming to add new  function  to  them.
  Approximately  80% of University Information  Systems  (UIS)
  resources  are  used to maintain and  enhance  the  existing
  systems.
 
  It would be extremely expensive to rewrite all of our exist-
  ing  systems from the point of view of time and  disruption.
  For   example,  it  would  take  at  least  five  to   seven
  programmer/analyst  person  years  to  rewrite  the  student
  records system, and while the new system was being developed
  nothing  but critical enhancements could be applied  to  the
  existing  system.   We  must  look  to  some  of   the   new
  developments   in   computer  aided   design   and   systems
  restructuring  software to re-engineer the existing  systems
  to   a  standard  design,  programming   and   documentation
  methodology.  Re-engineering our systems would significantly
  reduce  the complexity of the systems structure, and  reduce
  the  analysis  and  programming  effort  for  future  system
  enhancements.  At the  same time we must ensure that any new
  systems  that  are  developed adhere to the  same  data  and
  programming standards.
 
  How does/should the University view its data?  In the  early
  eighties  one  of  the  objectives  for  the  administrative
  systems    was    to   view   data   not   only    at    the
  operational/functional  level,  but also at  the  University
  level.  It was an objective that, where data was of interest
  to more than one operational unit, a University  perspective
  be  taken.  To some degree this objective has been met,  but
  the  issue  of  "University"  data  standards  needs  to  be
  reviewed  to determine how well the original  objective  has
  been  met  and  what corrective steps, if any,  need  to  be
  taken.
 
  Some  administrative  computing  is currently  done  on  the
  academic mainframe, as the academic system's software  "tool
  set" is more suited for analysis, end-user computing and  ad
  hoc  reporting.   As well, the distinction between  what  is
  academic  and what is administrative computing  is  becoming
  blurred.  In many instances this is advantageous in that  it
  provides  greater  flexibility  in  systems  planning;   for
  example:   expensive  software products could be  shared  by
  both  administrative  and academic users versus  copies  for
  each  environment.  While this is worthwhile it will  always
  be necessary to be able to identify the cost associated with
  the provision of services to each user group.
 
  To   determine   the  issues,  directions   and   priorities
  associated with the integration of the academic and adminis-
  trative computing environments, the University should estab-
  lish  a  new advisory committee.  In consultation  with  the
  SCCC and with representation from each principal administra-
  tive user department, its mandate will include the identifi-
  cation of the functional requirements for the development of
  the  University's data base environment, from the  viewpoint
  of integrating applications systems, and the  implementation
  of appropriate computer aided design and system  restructur-
  ing software tools.
 
 
 
  CONCLUSION:
  __________
 
  For nearly 150 years Queen's reputation for academic  excel-
  lence, creativity and spirit has influenced Canadian  higher
  education.   Today Queen's is recognized as  an  outstanding
  Canadian centre of higher learning and research.
 
  However, unless the University is prepared to place substan-
  tial   resources   into   supporting   computing,    Queen's
  researchers  will  not be able to  compete.   Many  American
  institutions are implementing systems to create new environ-
  ments to support research and teaching.  Already a difficult
  task, faculty recruitment  will become even more competitive
  as the peak retirement period arrives in the 1990s.  Failure
  of  Queen's  to  keep  pace  with  these  institutions  will
  severely  affect  our ability to attract the  best  faculty.
  Hence,   the  quality  of  our  research,  instruction   and
  graduates will ultimately be affected.
 
  We have the opportunity to develop Scholar-Centred Computing
  which  will provide the computing environment for the  1990s
  and ensure Queen's place among Canadian universities.

